47	/**
48	 * A builder of {@link LoadingCache} and {@link Cache} instances.
49	 *
50	 * <h2>Prefer <a href="https://github.com/ben-manes/caffeine/wiki">Caffeine</a> over Guava's caching
51	 * API</h2>
52	 *
53	 * <p>The successor to Guava's caching API is <a
54	 * href="https://github.com/ben-manes/caffeine/wiki">Caffeine</a>. Its API is designed to make it a
55	 * nearly drop-in replacement. Note that it is not available for Android or GWT/J2CL and that it may
56	 * have <a href="https://github.com/ben-manes/caffeine/wiki/Guava">different (usually better)
57	 * behavior</a> when multiple threads attempt concurrent mutations. Its equivalent to {@code
58	 * CacheBuilder} is its <a
59	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/caffeine/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/Caffeine.html">{@code
60	 * Caffeine}</a> class. Caffeine offers better performance, more features (including asynchronous
61	 * loading), and fewer <a
62	 * href="https://github.com/google/guava/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3Apackage%3Dcache+label%3Atype%3Ddefect">bugs</a>.
63	 *
64	 * <p>Caffeine defines its own interfaces (<a
65	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/caffeine/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/Cache.html">{@code
66	 * Cache}</a>, <a
67	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/caffeine/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/LoadingCache.html">{@code
68	 * LoadingCache}</a>, <a
69	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/caffeine/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/CacheLoader.html">{@code
70	 * CacheLoader}</a>, etc.), so you can use Caffeine without needing to use any Guava types.
71	 * Caffeine's types are better than Guava's, especially for <a
72	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/caffeine/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/AsyncLoadingCache.html">their
73	 * deep support for asynchronous operations</a>. But if you want to migrate to Caffeine with minimal
74	 * code changes, you can use <a
75	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/guava/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine.guava/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/guava/CaffeinatedGuava.html">its
76	 * {@code CaffeinatedGuava} adapter class</a>, which lets you build a Guava {@code Cache} or a Guava
77	 * {@code LoadingCache} backed by a Guava {@code CacheLoader}.
78	 *
79	 * <p>Caffeine's API for asynchronous operations uses {@code CompletableFuture}: <a
80	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/caffeine/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/AsyncLoadingCache.html#get(K)">{@code
81	 * AsyncLoadingCache.get}</a> returns a {@code CompletableFuture}, and implementations of <a
82	 * href="https://www.javadoc.io/doc/com.github.ben-manes.caffeine/caffeine/latest/com.github.benmanes.caffeine/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/AsyncCacheLoader.html#asyncLoad(K,java.util.concurrent.Executor)">{@code
83	 * AsyncCacheLoader.asyncLoad}</a> must return a {@code CompletableFuture}. Users of Guava's {@link
84	 * com.google.common.util.concurrent.ListenableFuture} can adapt between the two {@code Future}
85	 * types by using <a href="https://github.com/lukas-krecan/future-converter#java8-guava">{@code
86	 * net.javacrumbs.futureconverter.java8guava.FutureConverter}</a>.
87	 *
88	 * <h2>More on {@code CacheBuilder}</h2>
89	 *
90	 * {@code CacheBuilder} builds caches with any combination of the following features:
91	 *
92	 * <ul>
93	 *   <li>automatic loading of entries into the cache
94	 *   <li>least-recently-used eviction when a maximum size is exceeded (note that the cache is
95	 *       divided into segments, each of which does LRU internally)
96	 *   <li>time-based expiration of entries, measured since last access or last write
97	 *   <li>keys automatically wrapped in {@linkplain WeakReference weak} references
98	 *   <li>values automatically wrapped in {@linkplain WeakReference weak} or {@linkplain
99	 *       SoftReference soft} references
100	 *   <li>notification of evicted (or otherwise removed) entries
101	 *   <li>accumulation of cache access statistics
102	 * </ul>
103	 *
104	 * <p>These features are all optional; caches can be created using all or none of them. By default,
105	 * cache instances created by {@code CacheBuilder} will not perform any type of eviction.
106	 *
107	 * <p>Usage example:
108	 *
109	 * {@snippet :
110	 * LoadingCache<Key, Graph> graphs = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
111	 *     .maximumSize(10000)
112	 *     .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(10))
113	 *     .removalListener(MY_LISTENER)
114	 *     .build(
115	 *         new CacheLoader<Key, Graph>() {
116	 *           public Graph load(Key key) throws AnyException {
117	 *             return createExpensiveGraph(key);
118	 *           }
119	 *         });
120	 * }
121	 *
122	 * <p>Or equivalently,
123	 *
124	 * {@snippet :
125	 * // In real life this would come from a command-line flag or config file
126	 * String spec = "maximumSize=10000,expireAfterWrite=10m";
127	 *
128	 * LoadingCache<Key, Graph> graphs = CacheBuilder.from(spec)
129	 *     .removalListener(MY_LISTENER)
130	 *     .build(
131	 *         new CacheLoader<Key, Graph>() {
132	 *           public Graph load(Key key) throws AnyException {
133	 *             return createExpensiveGraph(key);
134	 *           }
135	 *         });
136	 * }
137	 *
138	 * <p>The returned cache implements all optional operations of the {@link LoadingCache} and {@link
139	 * Cache} interfaces. The {@code asMap} view (and its collection views) have <i>weakly consistent
140	 * iterators</i>. This means that they are safe for concurrent use, but if other threads modify the
141	 * cache after the iterator is created, it is undefined which of these changes, if any, are
142	 * reflected in that iterator. These iterators never throw {@link ConcurrentModificationException}.
143	 *
144	 * <p><b>Note:</b> by default, the returned cache uses equality comparisons (the {@link
145	 * Object#equals equals} method) to determine equality for keys or values. However, if {@link
146	 * #weakKeys} was specified, the cache uses identity ({@code ==}) comparisons instead for keys.
147	 * Likewise, if {@link #weakValues} or {@link #softValues} was specified, the cache uses identity
148	 * comparisons for values.
149	 *
150	 * <p>Entries are automatically evicted from the cache when any of {@link #maximumSize(long)
151	 * maximumSize}, {@link #maximumWeight(long) maximumWeight}, {@link #expireAfterWrite
152	 * expireAfterWrite}, {@link #expireAfterAccess expireAfterAccess}, {@link #weakKeys weakKeys},
153	 * {@link #weakValues weakValues}, or {@link #softValues softValues} are requested.
154	 *
155	 * <p>If {@link #maximumSize(long) maximumSize} or {@link #maximumWeight(long) maximumWeight} is
