431	  /**
432	   * Guides the allowed concurrency among update operations. Used as a hint for internal sizing. The
433	   * table is internally partitioned to try to permit the indicated number of concurrent updates
434	   * without contention. Because assignment of entries to these partitions is not necessarily
435	   * uniform, the actual concurrency observed may vary. Ideally, you should choose a value to
436	   * accommodate as many threads as will ever concurrently modify the table. Using a significantly
437	   * higher value than you need can waste space and time, and a significantly lower value can lead
438	   * to thread contention. But overestimates and underestimates within an order of magnitude do not
439	   * usually have much noticeable impact. A value of one permits only one thread to modify the cache
440	   * at a time, but since read operations and cache loading computations can proceed concurrently,
441	   * this still yields higher concurrency than full synchronization.
442	   *
443	   * <p>Defaults to 4. <b>Note:</b>The default may change in the future. If you care about this
444	   * value, you should always choose it explicitly.
445	   *
446	   * <p>The current implementation uses the concurrency level to create a fixed number of hashtable
447	   * segments, each governed by its own write lock. The segment lock is taken once for each explicit
448	   * write, and twice for each cache loading computation (once prior to loading the new value, and
449	   * once after loading completes). Much internal cache management is performed at the segment
450	   * granularity. For example, access queues and write queues are kept per segment when they are
451	   * required by the selected eviction algorithm. As such, when writing unit tests it is not
452	   * uncommon to specify {@code concurrencyLevel(1)} in order to achieve more deterministic eviction
453	   * behavior.
454	   *
455	   * <p>Note that future implementations may abandon segment locking in favor of more advanced
456	   * concurrency controls.
457	   *
458	   * @return this {@code CacheBuilder} instance (for chaining)
459	   * @throws IllegalArgumentException if {@code concurrencyLevel} is nonpositive
460	   * @throws IllegalStateException if a concurrency level was already set
461	   */
462	  @CanIgnoreReturnValue
463	  public CacheBuilder<K, V> concurrencyLevel(int concurrencyLevel) {
464	    checkState(
465	        this.concurrencyLevel == UNSET_INT,
466	        "concurrency level was already set to %s",
467	        this.concurrencyLevel);
468	    checkArgument(concurrencyLevel > 0);
469	    this.concurrencyLevel = concurrencyLevel;
470	    return this;
471	  }
472	
473	  int getConcurrencyLevel() {
474	    return (concurrencyLevel == UNSET_INT) ? DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY_LEVEL : concurrencyLevel;
475	  }

